---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import DevFormatJsonCode from '@/components/tools/dev/DevFormatJsonCode.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="json-to-zod-schema">
  <DevFormatJsonCode client:load target="zod" />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>Why a schema beats an interface at a boundary</h2>
    <p>
      A TypeScript interface is a compile-time assertion. Write
      <code>const user = await res.json() as User</code> and the compiler believes you. At runtime, nothing
      is checked. When the API drops a field, the failure shows as
      <code>Cannot read properties of undefined</code> in an unrelated place, minutes or days later.
    </p>
    <p>
      A Zod schema is a runtime check that also gives the static type. <code>userSchema.parse(data)</code>
      throws at once, at the boundary, with a path to the bad field. <code>z.infer&lt;typeof userSchema&gt;</code>
      gives the same <code>User</code> type you would write by hand. One definition gives both.
    </p>

    <h2>What the generator emits</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>One <code>z.object(&#123;…&#125;)</code> per distinct nested shape, named after the key that holds it.</li>
      <li><code>.optional()</code> on keys that were absent from some records.</li>
      <li><code>.nullable()</code> where a null was observed alongside another type.</li>
      <li><code>z.number().int()</code> for values that were whole numbers, <code>z.number()</code> where any sample was fractional.</li>
      <li><code>z.array(…)</code> with the merged element schema, or <code>z.unknown()</code> if the array was empty.</li>
      <li>A final <code>z.infer</code> type alias for the root.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The JSON to Zod generator declares child schemas before the schemas that reference them, so the file
      compiles without reordering. It imports only <code>zod</code>.
    </p>

    <h2>Tightening the generated schema</h2>
    <p>
      The output has the correct structure but loose meaning, because no sample can show intent. Add these
      refinements by hand:
    </p>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Generated</th><th>Better</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>z.string()</code> for an email</td><td><code>z.string().email()</code></td><td>Catches malformed addresses at the boundary</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>z.string()</code> for a status</td><td><code>z.enum(['open','closed','pending'])</code></td><td>Turns an unexpected value into an error, and narrows the type</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>z.string()</code> for a timestamp</td><td><code>z.string().datetime()</code> or <code>z.coerce.date()</code></td><td>Validates the format, or gives you a Date</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>z.number()</code> for a quantity</td><td><code>z.number().int().nonnegative()</code></td><td>Encodes the real constraint</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>z.object(&#123;…&#125;)</code></td><td><code>.strict()</code></td><td>Rejects unknown keys instead of stripping them</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>Handling failures well</h2>
    <p>
      At a network boundary, use <code>safeParse</code>, not <code>parse</code>. It returns a discriminated
      union and does not throw, so you can log <code>result.error.issues</code> (with the exact path,
      expected type and received type) and show the user a clear message, not a stack trace. In a React
      app, <code>z.treeifyError</code> maps the issues onto form fields directly.
    </p>

    <h2>Schema drift</h2>
    <p>
      A runtime schema mostly catches changes made by other people. A provider renames
      <code>created_at</code> to <code>createdAt</code>, starts to return an ID as a number, or drops a
      field they thought nobody used. With a schema at the boundary, you get one clear error that names
      the field. Without one, you get a support ticket about a blank column.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
